    "question": {
      ":question/id": "2ff3cf02-f7f1-55c8-b45e-69b5f86f6b48",
      ":question/problem": "Find $f(x,y,z)$ such that $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z)&=\\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathbf{i} \\\\ &{}+\\left(- x + 2 z\\right)\\,\\mathbf{j} \\\\ &{}+\\left(2 y - 2\\right)\\,\\mathbf{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The gradient of the function $f(x,y,z)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathbf{j}+\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\,\\mathbf{k}.\n$$\n\nSince $\\nabla f(x,y,z)=\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z),$ the function $f(x,y,z)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}&=- y \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}&=- x + 2 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}&=2 y - 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$ and $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=\\int \\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\\\ &=- x y+g(y,z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, differentiating the above expression with respect to $y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nTaking $\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}=- x + 2 z$ into account, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}- x+\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=- x + 2 z \\\\ \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}&=2 z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate $\\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}$ with respect to $y.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $z:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(y,z)&=\\int \\frac{\\partial g}{\\partial y}\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=\\int \\left(2 z\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y \\\\ &=2 y z+h(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $g(y,z)=2 y z+h(z)$ in the expression for $f(x,y,z),$ we get\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- x y + 2 y z+h(z).\n$$\n\nTo determine the function $h(z),$ we differentiate $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $z.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}=2 y+h'(z).\n$$\n\nComparing this derivative with the third component and subtracting the known terms gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h'(z)&=-2 \\\\ h(z)&=- 2 z+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is a constant.\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=- x y + 2 y z - 2 z+C.\n$$",
